From ad9a3e94849ae9d01a246ab0e033e8eaec4be5f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:31:12 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 52/53] antinomie: show a desktop or laptop machine's devices
 for QEMU's

A desktop or laptop machine profile gives the VM a real machine's
firmware strings, but programs still read QEMU's own devices: the Bochs
display (1234:1111), Red Hat's subsystem 1af4 on every chipset device,
QEMU's NVMe controller ("QEMU NVMe Ctrl", QEMU's version as firmware, an
org.qemu subsystem name, the 52:54:00 EUI-64), QEMU's ACPI creator ID BXPC
and OVMF's INTEL/EDK2 BGRT, the fw_cfg device in the DSDT, the bochs-drm
driver and framebuffer names, "QEMU USB Tablet" and OVMF's EFI vendor.

kernel/antinomie/facade.c (CONFIG_ANTINOMIE_FACADE) shows the profile's
values in those views when the host passes them as boot options, which
ant_cmdline_take() removes from the command line before it is printed:

- antinomie_fpci and antinomie_fsub: PCI rules (an emulated vendor:device
  shown as another, with its subsystem) and the board's subsystem for
  devices that carry QEMU's. pci_user_read_config_*() (sysfs config,
  /proc/bus/pci/BB/DD.F), the sysfs vendor, device, subsystem_* and
  modalias attributes, the uevent file as read, /proc/bus/pci/devices and
  the boot log's device line use them.
- antinomie_fnvme: model, firmware, OUI, VID and SSVID. Identify
  Controller (with a subsystem NQN as nvme_init_subnqn() makes one),
  Identify Namespace and the namespace descriptors (QEMU's EUI-64 prefix)
  as the driver reads them and as admin pass-through (NVME_IOCTL_ADMIN_CMD
  and ADMIN64, used by nvme-cli and smartctl) returns them, and the
  firmware slot log.
- antinomie_facpi: creator ID and revision, OEM and OEM table IDs. Tables
  read through /sys/firmware/acpi/tables and the boot log's table lines:
  BXPC becomes the creator, QEMU's and OVMF's OEM IDs the machine's, the
  DSDT's FWCF device an unused name with EisaId("PNP0C02") for its
  QEMU0002 _HID (padded with Noop); the checksum is recomputed.
- antinomie_fdrm: i915 or amdgpu. bochs-drm's PCI driver name (sysfs,
  log), its I/O resource names, DRM_IOCTL_VERSION's name, description and
  version, and the fbdev name (/proc/fb).
- antinomie_finput: a mouse's IDs, release and strings, given to QEMU's
  USB HID devices (vendor 0627, maker QEMU) right after the hub reads
  their descriptors, before a driver binds: sysfs, the descriptors file
  and the input device's name, IDs and (no) serial follow.
- antinomie_fefi: the firmware vendor of the boot log's EFI line.
- antinomie_fnocd: the machine has no optical drive, so QEMU's default
  CD-ROM (q35 adds one when no IDE disk is given, as with an NVMe root) is
  disabled in ata_dev_configure() before it is attached.

The DRM core's and bochs's log lines name the shown driver too.

The kernel keeps the true identities everywhere else: drivers bind and
quirks apply by them, and the uevents the kernel sends carry them, so
udev loads the emulated devices' modules. Root's raw config-space access
through port I/O, and USB string requests made through usbfs, still read
QEMU's values.

Hooked kernel files: drivers/pci/{access.c,pci-sysfs.c,proc.c,probe.c,
pci-driver.c}, drivers/base/core.c (uevent_show), kernel/resource.c,
drivers/nvme/host/{core.c,ioctl.c}, drivers/acpi/sysfs.c,
drivers/acpi/acpica/tbprint.c, drivers/gpu/drm/{drm_ioctl.c,
drm_fb_helper.c,drm_drv.c,tiny/bochs.c}, drivers/usb/core/hub.c,
drivers/firmware/efi/efi.c, drivers/ata/libata-core.c.
pci_read_bridge_windows() (probe.c) is __always_inline, as Debian's build
inlines it: the log line's hook made pci_setup_device() larger, and an
out-of-line copy is a BTF and traceable function Debian's kernel lacks.
On a stock-profile kernel (Debian bases) only the built-in hooks act:
NVMe, DRM, USB and libata are Debian's own modules there.
ant_acpi_table_shown() takes a plain struct acpi_table_header pointer: a
const one would be a BTF type Debian's kernel lacks. facade.c logs
nothing on a failed copy back: a dynamic debug entry would name it in
/proc/dynamic_debug/control, which every user can read.

Contract revision 21 (no protocol change; bases with the facade list
"facade" in stealth_support).
